      "section_type": "summary",
      "id": 2,
      "title": "Chapter Summary",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "In this chapter, we explored the fundamentals of trigonometry. We learned that trigonometry is the study of the relationship between the sides and angles of a right-angled triangle. We identified the three sides relative to an acute angle: the Hypotenuse (the longest side), the Opposite side (across from the angle), and the Adjacent side (next to the angle). We defined the six key trigonometric ratios: Sine (Opposite/Hypotenuse), Cosine (Adjacent/Hypotenuse), and Tangent (Opposite/Adjacent), often remembered by the mnemonic SOH CAH TOA. We also defined their reciprocal ratios: Cosecant (1/Sine), Secant (1/Cosine), and Cotangent (1/Tangent). Finally, we saw how the Pythagorean theorem, a² + b² = c², is essential for finding an unknown side length, allowing us to calculate all six trigonometric ratios even when only two sides are known.",
