      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 3,
      "title": "Trigonometric Ratios",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "To understand trigonometric ratios, we must first start with a special kind of triangle: the right-angled triangle. This is a triangle where one of the angles is exactly 90 degrees. The sides of this triangle have special names that are always defined relative to one of the other two angles, which we'll call angle 'A' for our example. The longest side, which is always opposite the right angle, is called the Hypotenuse. It's the 'sloped' side of the triangle. The side directly across from our angle A is called the Opposite side. And the remaining side, which is next to angle A but isn't the hypotenuse, is called the Adjacent side. 'Adjacent' just means 'next to'. These three names—Opposite, Adjacent, and Hypotenuse—are the building blocks for our ratios. There are six fundamental trigonometric ratios, which are simply specific fractions formed by the lengths of these three sides. The first three are Sine, Cosine, and Tangent. The sine of angle A, written as sin A, is the ratio of the length of the Opposite side to the length of the Hypotenuse. The cosine of angle A, or cos A, is the ratio of the Adjacent side to the Hypotenuse. And the tangent of angle A, or tan A, is the ratio of the Opposite side to the Adjacent side. We'll look at the other three ratios, their reciprocals, in a moment.",
