    {
      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 4,
      "title": "Inverse of Trigonometric Ratios",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Now that we've covered the three primary trigonometric ratios—Sine, Cosine, and Tangent—let's explore the other three. These are called the reciprocal ratios: Cosecant, Secant, and Cotangent. The term 'reciprocal' is just a mathematical way of saying 'one divided by', or more simply, the 'flipped' version of a fraction. Each reciprocal ratio is paired with one of the primary ratios. Cosecant, written as csc A, is the reciprocal of sin A. So, if sin A is Opposite over Hypotenuse, then csc A is simply Hypotenuse over Opposite. We just flip the fraction. Next is Secant, written as sec A, which is the reciprocal of cos A. If cos A is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, then sec A becomes Hypotenuse over Adjacent. Again, we just flip the fraction. Finally, we have Cotangent, or cot A. This is the reciprocal of tan A. If tan A is Opposite over Adjacent, then cot A is Adjacent over Opposite. Understanding these reciprocal pairs is extremely helpful. It means you don't need to memorize six separate formulas. If you know SOH CAH TOA for Sine, Cosine, and Tangent, you can always find Cosecant, Secant, and Cotangent just by flipping the fractions. This simple trick will be invaluable as you start solving trigonometric problems.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "Now that we've covered the three primary trigonometric ratios—Sine, Cosine, and Tangent—let's explore the other three. These are called the reciprocal ratios: Cosecant, Secant, and Cotangent.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "The term 'reciprocal' is just a mathematical way of saying 'one divided by', or more simply, the 'flipped' version of a fraction. Each reciprocal ratio is paired with one of the primary ratios.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "Cosecant, written as csc A, is the reciprocal of sin A. So, if sin A is Opposite over Hypotenuse, then csc A is simply Hypotenuse over Opposite. We just flip the fraction.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Next is Secant, written as sec A, which is the reciprocal of cos A. If cos A is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, then sec A becomes Hypotenuse over Adjacent. Again, we just flip the fraction.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "Finally, we have Cotangent, or cot A. This is the reciprocal of tan A. If tan A is Opposite over Adjacent, then cot A is Adjacent over Opposite.",
          "duration": 11
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "text": "Understanding these reciprocal pairs is extremely helpful. It means you don't need to memorize six separate formulas. If you know SOH CAH TOA for Sine, Cosine, and Tangent, you can always find Cosecant, Secant, and Cotangent just by flipping the fractions. This simple trick will be invaluable as you start solving trigonometric problems.",
          "duration": 20
        }
      ],
