    {
      "section_type": "example",
      "id": 6,
      "title": "Example 2: Prove ∠B = ∠Q from sin B = sin Q",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "This example is a bit more abstract and involves a proof. We are given two acute angles, B and Q, such that sin B is equal to sin Q. Our task is to prove that angle B must be equal to angle Q. Let's start by considering two separate right-angled triangles: triangle ABC, right-angled at C, and triangle PQR, right-angled at R. The given condition is sin B = sin Q. In triangle ABC, sin B is Opposite over Hypotenuse, which is AC over AB. In triangle PQR, sin Q is PR over PQ. So our equation is AC/AB = PR/PQ. Let's rearrange this slightly by swapping AB and PR. This gives us AC/PR = AB/PQ. This shows that the ratio of corresponding sides of the two triangles is constant. Let's call this constant 'k'. So, AC = k * PR and AB = k * PQ. Now, let's find the third side of each triangle using Pythagoras. For triangle ABC, the side BC is the square root of AB squared minus AC squared. For triangle PQR, the side QR is the square root of PQ squared minus PR squared. Let's substitute our 'k' expressions into the equation for BC. BC becomes the square root of (k*PQ) squared minus (k*PR) squared. We can factor out k-squared from under the root, which becomes k outside the root. This leaves us with BC = k times the square root of (PQ squared minus PR squared). Now look at the ratio of the third sides, BC over QR. It's k times the square root expression divided by the same square root expression. They cancel out, leaving us with BC/QR = k. This is amazing! We've now shown that AC/PR = AB/PQ = BC/QR. All three pairs of corresponding sides are in the same ratio. By the Side-Side-Side (SSS) similarity criterion, this means that triangle ABC is similar to triangle PQR. And if two triangles are similar, their corresponding angles must be equal. Therefore, angle B must be equal to angle Q, and our proof is complete.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "This example is a bit more abstract and involves a proof. We are given two acute angles, B and Q, such that sin B is equal to sin Q. Our task is to prove that angle B must be equal to angle Q.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "Let's start by considering two separate right-angled triangles: triangle ABC, right-angled at C, and triangle PQR, right-angled at R.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "The given condition is sin B = sin Q. In triangle ABC, sin B is Opposite over Hypotenuse, which is AC over AB. In triangle PQR, sin Q is PR over PQ. So our equation is AC/AB = PR/PQ.",
          "duration": 16
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Let's rearrange this slightly by swapping AB and PR. This gives us AC/PR = AB/PQ. This shows that the ratio of corresponding sides of the two triangles is constant. Let's call this constant 'k'.",
          "duration": 15
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "So, AC = k * PR and AB = k * PQ. Now, let's find the third side of each triangle using Pythagoras.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "text": "For triangle ABC, the side BC is the square root of AB squared minus AC squared. For triangle PQR, the side QR is the square root of PQ squared minus PR squared.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "text": "Let's substitute our 'k' expressions into the equation for BC. BC becomes the square root of (k*PQ) squared minus (k*PR) squared.",
          "duration": 10
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "text": "We can factor out k-squared from under the root, which becomes k outside the root. This leaves us with BC = k times the square root of (PQ squared minus PR squared).",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "text": "Now look at the ratio of the third sides, BC over QR. It's k times the square root expression divided by the same square root expression. They cancel out, leaving us with BC/QR = k.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "text": "This is amazing! We've now shown that AC/PR = AB/PQ = BC/QR. All three pairs of corresponding sides are in the same ratio.",
          "duration": 11
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "text": "By the Side-Side-Side (SSS) similarity criterion, this means that triangle ABC is similar to triangle PQR. And if two triangles are similar, their corresponding angles must be equal. Therefore, angle B must be equal to angle Q, and our proof is complete.",
          "duration": 18
        }
      ],
