      "section_type": "example",
      "id": 7,
      "title": "Example 3: Trigonometric Identity",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Let's explore a very important trigonometric identity with this example. Consider a right-angled triangle ACB, where the right angle is at C. We're given that the hypotenuse AB is 29 units, and side BC is 21 units. The angle ABC is labeled as theta. Our task is to determine the values of two expressions: first, cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta, and second, cos-squared theta minus sin-squared theta. Before we can do anything, we need to find the length of the third side, AC. We use the Pythagorean theorem: AC equals the square root of AB squared minus BC squared. Plugging in the numbers, AC is the square root of 29 squared minus 21 squared. 29 squared is 841, and 21 squared is 441. The difference is 400. So, AC is the square root of 400, which is 20. Now we have all three sides: AC (Opposite to theta) is 20, BC (Adjacent to theta) is 21, and AB (Hypotenuse) is 29. Let's find sin theta and cos theta. Sin theta is Opposite over Hypotenuse, so it's 20 over 29. Cos theta is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, so it's 21 over 29. Now for the first part: cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta. This is (21/29) squared plus (20/29) squared. This equals 441 over 841 plus 400 over 841. Adding them together gives 841 over 841, which is exactly 1. This is a fundamental identity in trigonometry: for any angle theta, cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta is always 1. For the second part, cos-squared theta minus sin-squared theta. This is (21/29) squared minus (20/29) squared, which is 441 over 841 minus 400 over 841. The result is 41 over 841. This demonstrates how we can evaluate complex trigonometric expressions once we know the side lengths of the triangle.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "Let's explore a very important trigonometric identity with this example. Consider a right-angled triangle ACB, where the right angle is at C. We're given that the hypotenuse AB is 29 units, and side BC is 21 units. The angle ABC is labeled as theta.",
          "duration": 17
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "Our task is to determine the values of two expressions: first, cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta, and second, cos-squared theta minus sin-squared theta.",
          "duration": 11
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "Before we can do anything, we need to find the length of the third side, AC. We use the Pythagorean theorem: AC equals the square root of AB squared minus BC squared.",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Plugging in the numbers, AC is the square root of 29 squared minus 21 squared. 29 squared is 841, and 21 squared is 441. The difference is 400. So, AC is the square root of 400, which is 20.",
          "duration": 16
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "Now we have all three sides: AC (Opposite to theta) is 20, BC (Adjacent to theta) is 21, and AB (Hypotenuse) is 29.",
          "duration": 10
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "text": "Let's find sin theta and cos theta. Sin theta is Opposite over Hypotenuse, so it's 20 over 29. Cos theta is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, so it's 21 over 29.",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "text": "Now for the first part: cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta. This is (21/29) squared plus (20/29) squared.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "text": "This equals 441 over 841 plus 400 over 841. Adding them together gives 841 over 841, which is exactly 1.",
          "duration": 10
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "text": "This is a fundamental identity in trigonometry: for any angle theta, cos-squared theta plus sin-squared theta is always 1.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "text": "For the second part, cos-squared theta minus sin-squared theta. This is (21/29) squared minus (20/29) squared, which is 441 over 841 minus 400 over 841. The result is 41 over 841.",
          "duration": 16
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "text": "This demonstrates how we can evaluate complex trigonometric expressions once we know the side lengths of the triangle.",
          "duration": 6
        }
      ],
