      "section_type": "example",
      "id": 9,
      "title": "Example 5: Find sin Q and cos Q",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "In our final example, we have a triangle OPQ, which is right-angled at P. We are given that the side OP is 7 cm long. We're also given a relationship between the other two sides: the hypotenuse OQ minus the side PQ is equal to 1 cm. Our goal is to find the values of sin Q and cos Q. From the given relationship, OQ - PQ = 1, we can express the hypotenuse OQ as 1 + PQ. This will be very useful. Now, let's apply the Pythagorean theorem to triangle OPQ. The square of the hypotenuse, OQ squared, equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides, OP squared plus PQ squared. Now we can substitute what we know into this equation. We replace OQ with (1 + PQ) and OP with 7. The equation becomes (1 + PQ) squared equals 7 squared plus PQ squared. Let's expand the left side: 1 squared is 1, 2 times 1 times PQ is 2PQ, and PQ squared is PQ squared. So we have 1 + 2PQ + PQ squared equals 49 + PQ squared. Notice that PQ squared appears on both sides of the equation, so we can cancel them out. This simplifies things greatly, leaving us with 1 + 2PQ = 49. Now we can solve for PQ. Subtracting 1 from both sides gives 2PQ = 48. Dividing by 2, we find that PQ = 24 cm. Now that we have PQ, we can easily find OQ using our earlier relationship: OQ = 1 + PQ, which is 1 + 24, so OQ = 25 cm. We have all three sides: OP = 7, PQ = 24, and OQ = 25. Finally, we can find sin Q and cos Q. For angle Q, the opposite side is OP and the adjacent side is PQ. So, sin Q, which is Opposite over Hypotenuse, is OP/OQ or 7 over 25. And cos Q, which is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, is PQ/OQ or 24 over 25.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "In our final example, we have a triangle OPQ, which is right-angled at P. We are given that the side OP is 7 cm long.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "We're also given a relationship between the other two sides: the hypotenuse OQ minus the side PQ is equal to 1 cm. Our goal is to find the values of sin Q and cos Q.",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "From the given relationship, OQ - PQ = 1, we can express the hypotenuse OQ as 1 + PQ. This will be very useful.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Now, let's apply the Pythagorean theorem to triangle OPQ. The square of the hypotenuse, OQ squared, equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides, OP squared plus PQ squared.",
          "duration": 13
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "Now we can substitute what we know into this equation. We replace OQ with (1 + PQ) and OP with 7. The equation becomes (1 + PQ) squared equals 7 squared plus PQ squared.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "text": "Let's expand the left side: 1 squared is 1, 2 times 1 times PQ is 2PQ, and PQ squared is PQ squared. So we have 1 + 2PQ + PQ squared equals 49 + PQ squared.",
          "duration": 14
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "text": "Notice that PQ squared appears on both sides of the equation, so we can cancel them out. This simplifies things greatly, leaving us with 1 + 2PQ = 49.",
          "duration": 11
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "text": "Now we can solve for PQ. Subtracting 1 from both sides gives 2PQ = 48. Dividing by 2, we find that PQ = 24 cm.",
          "duration": 10
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "text": "Now that we have PQ, we can easily find OQ using our earlier relationship: OQ = 1 + PQ, which is 1 + 24, so OQ = 25 cm.",
          "duration": 11
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "text": "We have all three sides: OP = 7, PQ = 24, and OQ = 25. Finally, we can find sin Q and cos Q.",
          "duration": 9
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "text": "For angle Q, the opposite side is OP and the adjacent side is PQ. So, sin Q, which is Opposite over Hypotenuse, is OP/OQ or 7 over 25. And cos Q, which is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, is PQ/OQ or 24 over 25.",
          "duration": 17
        }
