          "scene": 1,
          "concept_title": "The Right-Angled Triangle",
          "description": "The foundation of trigonometry is the right-angled triangle. Its sides have special names relative to an angle.",
          "wan_prompt": "Flat vector illustration, bright colors, Indian educational textbook style, purple and blue gradient background. A friendly cartoon architect character stands next to a large glowing blueprint of a right-angled triangle. As the architect points, the sides light up one by one with labels: 'Hypotenuse' (the longest, sloped side), 'Opposite' (the side across from the angle), and 'Adjacent' (the side next to the angle). An eye icon sits at the angle to show the perspective.",
          "narration": "Everything in basic trigonometry begins with the right-angled triangle. It's our stage. For any chosen angle, which we call theta, the sides get special names. The longest side, always opposite the 90-degree corner, is the Hypotenuse. The side directly across from our angle theta is the Opposite. And the side right next to theta, that isn't the hypotenuse, is the Adjacent. Mastering these three names is the first crucial step to understanding all the ratios."
