          "wan_prompt": "Flat vector illustration, bright colors, Indian educational textbook style, purple and blue gradient background. A right-angled triangle has two sides labeled '3' and '4'. A question mark is on the hypotenuse. A magical animation shows a 3x3 square and a 4x4 square of blocks. The blocks then flow and reassemble into a single 5x5 square on the hypotenuse, revealing the missing side length is '5'. The formula a²+b²=c² glows brightly.",
          "narration": "What happens if you only know two sides of a right-angled triangle? You can always find the third using the ancient and powerful Pythagorean theorem. It states that if you square the lengths of the two shorter sides, 'a' and 'b', their sum will equal the square of the hypotenuse, 'c'. This formula, a-squared plus b-squared equals c-squared, is your ultimate tool for filling in the blanks. It’s essential for solving almost any trigonometry problem where a side is missing."
